e sources month by month in the country's own currency, then takes off the part of the banks' own funding that is not money (their capital, and deposits, bonds and loans the country does not count as money), so the rows add up to broad money. Other items, net, is the survey's balancing line (provisions, payments on their way between banks, valuation differences), shown with the sign that makes the rows add up. Money and credit are two sides of the same balance sheet, so this is never added to a credit or debt figure. The IMF counts money the same way in every country, which can be wider than the country's own measure of money. Source: International Monetary Fund."}